{#
  The catch-all production error page — everything that is not a 404, which has
  its own (error404.html.twig).

  Until now only the 404 was styled, so a production 500 fell through to
  Symfony's own grey page: no header, no way back, and nothing telling the
  reader whether the fault was theirs. This says the honest thing — the failure
  is ours, it has been logged, and here is the way back to the atlas.

  Deliberately NOT extending base.html.twig. This template renders when
  something has already gone wrong, and the base layout pulls in the nav, the
  account chip and the locale switcher — every one of which can touch the
  database or the session. A 500 page that itself 500s shows the reader a blank
  screen, so this one depends on nothing but its own markup.

  For the same reason the copy is inlined in English rather than translated:
  reaching the translator means booting more of the container, and the whole
  point of this page is to need as little of it as possible.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex">
  <title>Cycling Commons — something broke</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('styles/page/bundles/TwigBundle/Exception/error.css') }}">
</head>
<body>
  <section class="broke">
    <span class="code">{{ status_code }} · this one is on us</span>
    <h1>Something <em>broke</em>.</h1>
    <p>Not your doing, and nothing you sent has been lost. The failure has been logged and somebody will see it.</p>
    <p>Try again in a moment — or head back to the atlas.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <a class="p" href="/map">Open the map</a>
      <a class="g" href="/">Back to the atlas</a>
    </div>
    <div class="ref">If it keeps happening, tell us at info@cyclingcommons.org</div>
  </section>
</body>
</html>
